<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret Thatcher</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/2013">2013</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret Thatcher was a British Conservative politician and the first woman prime minister of the United Kingdom, serving from 1979 to 1990. Her governments privatised state industries, curbed trade union power, deregulated finance and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a> in 1982; her policies, known as Thatcherism, reshaped the British economy and divided opinion deeply.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Margaret Thatcher</b>'s government called all parties to Lancaster House in late 1979, where three months of talks nearly failed over land reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1979 the governments of <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> privatised state industries, curbed trade unions and deregulated the City of London, accelerating a shift from manufacturing to services and finance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Regional leaders could speak for themselves: during the British miners’ strike of 1984–85, when Wałęsa praised <b>Margaret Thatcher</b>, David Jastrzębski, president of Upper Silesia Solidarity, declared his support for the striking miners.</blockquote><a href="/movement/solidarity">Solidarity · People</a></li><li><blockquote>The post-war boom ended in the late 1960s and early 1970s with stagflation, and monetarism gained prominence under <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> (1979–1990) and Ronald Reagan (1981–1989), shifting attention from Keynes’s collectivist concerns to individual choice.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/capitalism">Capitalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1980s <b>Margaret Thatcher</b>’s government privatised large parts of the British economy, and the Reagan administration adopted neoliberalism, bringing 1980s growth and trillion-dollar deficits.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>After the economic crisis of the 1970s, <b>Margaret Thatcher</b> and Ronald Reagan turned from Keynesian economics to what became known as neoliberalism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li></ul></section>
